  "worked_solution": "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u20613$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20613) & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}\u20613)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20613) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(\\frac{2}{3})+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})+2(-36)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
